<ArticleLayout
  title="Flu vs. Cold: How to Tell the Difference"
  description="Can't tell if it's the flu or a common cold? Clear comparison of symptoms, onset, severity, and duration. Know the key differences and when to seek care."
  author="Dr. James Chen, PhD, MPH"
  reviewer="Dr. Sarah Mitchell, MD"
  publishDate="2026-07-28"
  lastReviewed="2026-07-28"
  illustration="/images/lungs.svg"
>
  <p>
    The flu and the common cold are both respiratory illnesses, but they're caused by different viruses.
    Knowing the difference matters — the flu can lead to serious complications, while colds rarely do.
    Here's how to tell them apart.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Quick Comparison Chart</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Symptom</th>
          <th>Influenza (Flu)</th>
          <th>Common Cold</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Onset</strong></td>
          <td>Sudden (hours)</td>
          <td>Gradual (days)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Fever</strong></td>
          <td>Common, high (100-104°F)</td>
          <td>Rare in adults, mild if present</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Body aches</strong></td>
          <td>Severe, widespread</td>
          <td>Mild or absent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Fatigue</strong></td>
          <td>Extreme, can last 2-3 weeks</td>
          <td>Mild</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Cough</strong></td>
          <td>Dry, persistent, can be severe</td>
          <td>Mild to moderate, often productive</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Headache</strong></td>
          <td>Common, often severe</td>
          <td>Uncommon</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Sneezing</strong></td>
          <td>Sometimes</td>
          <td>Common</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Sore throat</strong></td>
          <td>Sometimes</td>
          <td>Common</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Runny/stuffy nose</strong></td>
          <td>Sometimes</td>
          <td>Very common</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Complications</strong></td>
          <td>Pneumonia, hospitalization</td>
          <td>Rare (sinus infection, ear infection)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Duration</strong></td>
          <td>1-2 weeks (fatigue can linger)</td>
          <td>7-10 days</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

