  <h2>The "Symptom Severity Test"</h2>
  <p>A simple way to think about it:<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cold:</strong> You feel unwell but can usually function. Symptoms are "above the neck" — runny nose, sneezing, sore throat.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Flu:</strong> You feel too sick to function. Symptoms are "whole-body" — fever, severe aches, crushing fatigue. The thought of getting out of bed feels impossible.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-4" class="citation">[4]</a></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-info">
    <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> If symptoms came on <em>suddenly</em> and you feel like you've been "hit by a truck," it's more likely the flu.
    If symptoms crept up <em>gradually</em> over a day or two, it's more likely a cold.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Why It Matters</h2>
  <p>Distinguishing between flu and cold is important because:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antiviral treatment:</strong> Prescription antivirals (Tamiflu, Xofluza) work for flu — not for colds — and must be started within 48 hours<a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a><a href="#ref-5" class="citation">[5]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Complication risk:</strong> Flu can lead to pneumonia, especially in high-risk groups. Colds rarely do<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a></li>
    <li><strong>When to isolate:</strong> Flu requires more careful isolation to protect vulnerable people<a href="#ref-4" class="citation">[4]</a></li>
  </ul>

